# ChimeraX Script: Generate S348T and S348A mutants from the true 5I6Z chain A
# Run this in ChimeraX: File > Open > select this script
# Or from the ChimeraX command line: open generate_mutants.cxc
#
# Prerequisite: structural_audit.py must have been run first to produce
#               data/structures/5i6z_A_true.pdb

# 1. Load the true WT (chain A extracted from 5I6Z, ALA at position 348)
open ../data/structures/5i6z_A_true.pdb

# 2. Generate S348T mutant (ALA348 -> THR)
#    swapaa uses the best-fit rotamer from the Dunbrack library by default
swapaa thr #1:348
save ../data/structures/5i6z_A_S348T_correct.pdb #1 format pdb

# 3. Reload the WT and generate S348A mutant (ALA -> ALA is trivial,
#    but if the biological WT is meant to be SER, do SER->ALA here)
close #1
open ../data/structures/5i6z_A_true.pdb

# ALA is already at 348 in the true WT — S348A is functionally the same
# as true WT in this crystal structure context.
# If the intent was SER->ALA, use swapaa ala #1:348 from a SER-containing model.
# For documentation, save WT as the S348A equivalent:
save ../data/structures/5i6z_A_S348A_correct.pdb #1 format pdb

close #1

# 4. Visual comparison overlay
open ../data/structures/5i6z_A_true.pdb
open ../data/structures/5i6z_A_S348T_correct.pdb

matchmaker #2 to #1 alg bb
view :345-355

# Highlight residue 348 in both structures
color #1:348 cornflower blue
color #2:348 tomato
show #1:348 atoms
show #2:348 atoms

# Label
label #1:348 text "WT ALA348"
label #2:348 text "S348T THR348"

# Save comparison session
save ../data/structures/WT_vs_S348T_correct.cxs
